vine 1.2.0
prefix: readiness
title: Grimoire public release readiness
---
[readiness] Prepare Grimoire for public release (started)
Files: public repository metadata, documentation, examples, CLI, Scry-grounded measurement fixtures, CI, the static viewer, and the Rust package.
Do: Prepare the static Grimoire artifact for public publication without expanding its product boundary. Make the first-time experience workflow-first for two primary use cases: calculating and estimating costs and resources, and using a stable addressed substrate for computer-science architecture diagrams. Add a thin CLI as the machine-facing entry point and ground one public architecture sample with local Scry measurements. Keep the Grimoire MCP adapter deferred from this release; local Scry MCP remains development evidence only. Keep the specification, grammar, Rust implementation, executable fixtures, workflow guidance, and offline viewer mutually consistent. Keep runtime execution, continuous estimators, parameter updates, causal interventions, fixed points, compact indexed syntax, and richer network semantics as explicit boundaries.
Accept: A fresh clone has a clear entry point into both workflows, a portable CLI validation path, an explicit Scry-grounded sample, an explicit Grimoire MCP deferral, settled publication metadata, a canonical public example, green automated checks, an offline viewer, and no unexplained generated or machine-specific artifact.
-> readiness/handoff
> The public front door prioritizes explicit cost/resource analysis and stable architecture-substrate diagrams.
> The readiness plan is a local planning artifact; it is not itself a publication product or runtime dependency.
> The CLI is a thin machine-facing layer over the Rust library; it does not become a second semantic authority.
> The Grimoire MCP adapter is deferred from this release; local Scry MCP is development evidence only and is not a Grimoire runtime or publication dependency.
> Resources are tracked as typed, mutually non-interchangeable flows; FLOPs, bytes, memory, bandwidth, latency, and other kinds are not collapsed into one scalar.
> Probabilistic assumptions are explicit analysis inputs and qualified outputs; probabilities are not inferred from labels, topology, or hardware.
> The static boundary remains explicit: documentation and tools do not imply runtime training, sampling, control, or continuous estimation.
---
[readiness/handoff] Complete the human publication handoff (notstarted)
Files: repository settings and the selected remote.
Do: After the release gate passes, confirm the public repository owner, name, remote, default branch, and release posture. Push only the reviewed branch and create a release or tag only when the selected release posture calls for one. Keep credentials and private configuration out of the repository.
Accept: The public repository identity is explicit, the default branch renders the entry point, the selected license is visible, and the published tree contains only reviewed artifacts.
-> readiness/verify
---
[readiness/verify] Run the public release gate (notstarted)
Files: the tracked repository and the acceptance commands recorded here.
Do: Run `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`, `cargo test --workspace`, `cargo metadata --locked --no-deps`, the selected package check, `git diff --check`, repository-relative link checks, and direct offline viewer and CLI smoke checks. Exercise both primary workflows from the public front door, confirm the Grimoire MCP adapter remains deferred, and record command results and any remaining deferred boundary.
Accept: Every command passes; package metadata and contents are suitable for publication; both workflows are understandable from a fresh clone; CLI results agree with library results; the Grimoire MCP deferral is explicit; the viewer opens without a build step, server, dependency, or network request; no generated output, local database, credential, private path, or machine-specific artifact is tracked; and the final worktree contains only reviewed changes.
-> readiness/architecture-substrate
-> readiness/ci
-> readiness/cli
-> readiness/cost-resources
-> readiness/docs
-> readiness/example
-> readiness/hygiene
-> readiness/legal
-> readiness/metadata
-> readiness/readme
-> readiness/resource-flow
-> readiness/scope
-> readiness/scry-example
-> readiness/viewer
> Release verification tests explicit resource analysis and probabilistic assumptions separately from structural architecture behavior.
> CLI output is checked against the library/API results rather than accepted as an independent implementation.
> The Grimoire MCP adapter is not part of this release gate; its deferral is checked as a publication boundary rather than by an adapter smoke test.
> Unresolved runtime and continuous-analysis boundaries are recorded rather than hidden by a green presentation surface.
---
[readiness/scope] Set public identity and release posture (reviewing)
Files: the publication decisions consumed by metadata, public documentation, the two primary workflows, and the deferred adapter boundary.
Do: Record the public project identity and release posture, and record the product focus agreed for this branch. Confirm the public project name, repository owner and name, remote URL, package version policy, pre-release wording, license choice, and supported Rust toolchain policy. Record the local-only status of this readiness plan, the CLI boundary, the MCP deferral boundary, and the typed probabilistic resource direction. Record explicit human decisions where a value is not yet settled; downstream artifacts must not invent ownership, licensing, stability claims, or resource semantics.
Accept: Each publication identity and release-posture field has one explicit decision or an explicit human gate, the two primary workflows are recorded as the product focus, and the CLI/MCP/resource boundaries are unambiguous.
> The first-time user experience leads with cost/resource analysis and architecture-substrate diagrams.
> The readiness VINE remains local planning material and need not be published.
> The CLI is the primary machine-facing workflow surface and calls the library rather than duplicating semantics.
> The Grimoire MCP adapter is deferred from this release; any future inclusion is a separate gate from core language semantics.
> Resource kinds are mutually non-fungible and travel through the addressed graph as typed quantities or distributions; no universal exchange rate is assumed.
> Cost calculations use explicit caller-supplied structure, shape, axis, placement, and width inputs; probabilistic resource assumptions are explicit and qualified rather than label-inferred.
> The addressed graph and its layers are the source of architectural identity; diagrams are derived presentation views, not an alternate editable model.
> Unsettled repository ownership, remote, license, version, release-posture, and viewer-data-production values remain explicit human gates; Grimoire MCP inclusion is explicitly deferred for this release.
---
[readiness/metadata] Align package metadata (reviewing)
Files: `Cargo.toml` and `crates/grimoire/Cargo.toml`.
Do: Apply the settled public identity, description, license expression, repository or homepage metadata, package version, and Rust toolchain policy. Keep workspace inheritance valid and avoid metadata that claims a stable language contract without a corresponding decision. Check package metadata and package contents with Cargo.
Accept: Locked metadata reports the intended package identity; manifests contain no placeholder or private path; license and version match the settled posture; and the selected package check succeeds with the intended public files.
-> readiness/scope
---
[readiness/legal] Add the public license surface (notstarted)
Files: `LICENSE` and any narrowly required attribution file.
Do: Add the exact license text selected by the scope decision. Add attribution only where the repository's authorship or included material requires it, using verified names and sources. Keep paper citations in provenance and documentation rather than making ownership claims in the license file.
Accept: A fresh clone contains the complete selected license; manifests and README references agree with it; and no copyright holder or third-party permission is guessed.
-> readiness/scope
---
[readiness/readme] Maintain the public front door and workflow guide (started)
Files: `README.md` and `guidance/ml-project-workflow.md`.
Do: Keep the shortest useful front door for a new reader and make the two primary workflows immediately discoverable. Provide a cost/resource path that distinguishes deterministic calculation from sourced or probabilistic estimation, and an architecture-substrate path that explains addressed structure, layers, validation, canonical serialization, cuts, and diagram views. Document the CLI as the machine-facing path and document Grimoire MCP as deferred; keep local Scry MCP as private development evidence. Link to detailed contracts instead of duplicating their vocabulary or decisions.
Accept: A reader can choose either workflow, understand its inputs and boundaries, run the documented CLI checks, understand that Grimoire MCP is deferred without treating it as a core workflow, open the viewer locally, and navigate to authoritative documents, examples, fixtures, and license. Relative links resolve and the status wording matches the settled release posture.
-> readiness/cli
-> readiness/legal
-> readiness/mcp-detour
-> readiness/scope
> The README is a workflow front door, not a marketing landing page or a second specification.
> The two primary workflow paths are visible before secondary domain proposals.
> The workflow guide explains exact calculations versus probabilistic resource assumptions and stable model substrate versus diagram presentation.
> The CLI is documented as a thin library wrapper; Grimoire MCP is documented as deferred, while local Scry MCP remains development-only evidence with no independent Grimoire semantics.
> Detailed semantics remain owned by the specification, grammar, proposals, and executable API.
---
[readiness/example] Publish one canonical description (reviewing)
Files: `examples/reference.grimoire` and the reference fixture that validates it.
Do: Expose one canonical public description using the reviewed grammar. Choose one source of truth for the public example and executable fixture, or make their generation relationship mechanically obvious. Use the example to show stable addressed structure plus explicit inputs for cost/resource analysis, preserving layers, schema values, analysis inputs, and deferred boundaries without adding paper-specific core kinds or runtime behavior.
Accept: The public example parses, validates against the registry, evaluates its layers, round-trips through canonical serialization, and exercises a representative valid cut plus the visible non-cut failure. It provides a stable substrate for a diagram and a separate explicit-input analysis boundary. The example and test cannot silently diverge.
-> readiness/scope
> The canonical example demonstrates both primary workflows without making analysis values part of structural identity.
> The example remains one addressed description with layers, not two disconnected diagrams.
> Resource results use explicit analysis inputs and do not feed back into selection.
> The public example file is the source consumed directly by its integration test; the larger reference fixture remains separate coverage rather than a second copy of this example.
---
[readiness/docs] Audit public documentation (notstarted)
Files: `spec/`, `grammar/`, and `proposals/`.
Do: Review links, terminology, code snippets, authority boundaries, and publication-facing claims. Keep the core specification authoritative, keep the grammar and proposals honest about unresolved behavior, and make the two primary workflows, typed resource-flow direction, CLI boundary, and MCP deferral agree with the implementation. Remove stale internal wording only when repository evidence supports the change; do not settle an open design gap by prose alone.
Accept: Repository-relative links resolve; normative specification, grammar, design proposals, and workflow guidance are distinguishable; examples use the current API or grammar; resource kinds and probabilistic assumptions are not collapsed into unsupported scalar claims; and no document claims an unimplemented runtime or continuous-analysis contract.
-> readiness/resource-flow
-> readiness/scope
> Workflow guidance owns the first-time path; detailed proposals own their domain contracts.
> The docs audit checks that resource analysis never becomes hidden structural selection.
> The docs audit checks that architecture diagrams never become a competing source of structural identity.
> The docs audit checks that the CLI is the public adapter over the library and that the deferred MCP boundary introduces no parallel semantics.
