[readiness/architecture-substrate] Implement the architecture-substrate workflow (reviewing)
Files: `proposals/visualization.md`, `viz/index.html`, `crates/grimoire/src/model.rs`, `crates/grimoire/src/parser.rs`, `crates/grimoire/src/validate.rs`, `crates/grimoire/src/serialize.rs`, `crates/grimoire/src/cut.rs`, `crates/grimoire/src/evaluate.rs`, and the focused architecture fixtures.
Do: Make the second primary workflow usable as a stable substrate for computer-science architecture diagrams. Preserve exact addressed blocks, ports, connections, groups, layers, canonical serialization, validation, cuts, and evaluated views as the source model. Keep the diagram viewer read-only and compare reprojections by address; do not infer structure from labels, layout, overlays, or resource results and do not let the presentation become a competing authoring format. Coordinate with the CLI boundary so the same addressed model can be validated, canonicalized, evaluated, and consumed by tools.
Accept: A reader can define or consume a stable addressed description, validate and canonicalize it, derive a layer view or cut, and inspect that view as a diagram without losing identity. Structural changes are visible through model validation and serialization, while metadata, deterministic costs, and probabilistic resource overlays remain separate. Runtime execution and diagram editing remain outside the substrate.
-> readiness/scope
> Exact addresses are the identity key; human labels and diagram coordinates are presentation only.
> The core graph and layers are the portable model; diagrams are derived read-only views.
> Canonical serialization and downward-closed cuts are part of portability and review.
> The substrate represents static structure and finalized values, not runtime execution or training.
> Resource and MCP/CLI outputs cannot create, merge, or reinterpret addressed elements.
> The viewer's pure presentation function consumes explicit model and UI state and returns presentation data; DOM effects remain at the outer boundary.
@artifact text/html ./viz/index.html
@artifact text/rust ./crates/grimoire/tests/public_example.rs
---
[readiness/resource-flow] Define typed probabilistic resource flow (reviewing)
Files: the resource-flow proposal, the Rust resource model, focused resource fixtures, and the existing cost, placement, and measurement boundaries.
Do: Turn the resource direction into a bounded contract before expanding the implementation. Define machine-agnostic typed resources such as FLOP work, bytes, storage or memory, bandwidth, and latency as mutually non-interchangeable quantities. Define how resource quantities travel on addressed elements or relations, how nodes consume or transform them, how branches split them, and how explicit probabilistic assumptions qualify the resulting report. Keep route likelihood, workload distributions, resource-amount variation, and measurement uncertainty as distinct semantic roles rather than one generic probability field. Use an indexed-data/search pipeline as the motivating fixture without importing a neighboring project's private vocabulary or paths.
Accept: The contract names resource identity, units, flow and transformation rules, branch semantics, probability normalization, assumption provenance, report shape, and visible failure modes for missing inputs, invalid assumptions, unsupported conversions, and overflow. A fixture exercises multiple resource kinds and at least one conditional or branching assumption. The contract keeps exact calculations, probabilistic analysis, sourced measurements, and structural selection distinct.
-> readiness/scope
> Resource kinds are typed and mutually non-interchangeable; no universal scalar exchange rate is introduced.
> Resource flows are attached to addressed graph elements or relations and use explicit transformation/consumption rules.
> Probabilistic assumptions are explicit inputs with qualified outputs; topology, labels, and hardware do not supply them.
> Route likelihood, workload mix, resource-amount variation, and measurement uncertainty are distinct roles and cannot be collapsed into one probability field.
> The first bounded regime is static, finite, and acyclic; runtime schedulers and hardware behavior remain deferred.
> The indexed-data/search pipeline is a motivating fixture, not new core vocabulary.
> The neighboring indexed-artifact pattern informs separate accounting for model, index, checkpoint, residual, access, and integrity work; it does not become a dependency or copied vocabulary.
> The local Scry corpus is a development evidence source only; it is not a Grimoire runtime or publication dependency.
@artifact text/rust ./crates/grimoire/src/resource.rs
@artifact text/rust ./crates/grimoire/tests/resource_flow.rs
@guidance text/markdown ./proposals/resource-flow.md
---
[readiness/cli] Add the thin CLI workflow surface (reviewing)
Files: the Grimoire package manifest, CLI/library boundary, command tests, README, and workflow guide.
Do: Add a small machine-facing CLI over the Rust library. Choose the minimum command surface needed to validate and canonicalize descriptions, inspect or evaluate addressed layers and cuts, and run deterministic and typed probabilistic resource reports with explicit inputs. Provide human-readable output for first-time use and stable machine-readable output for automation, generated viewer payloads, and any future adapter. Keep file or standard-input handling, exit failures, offline operation, and no-runtime semantics explicit.
Accept: A fresh checkout can run both primary workflows through documented CLI commands; CLI results agree with direct library calls; malformed descriptions, missing layers, missing analysis inputs, invalid assumptions, and unsupported operations fail with stable visible errors; no CLI code duplicates parser, evaluator, serializer, cut, or resource semantics; and the CLI makes no network or runtime execution claims.
-> readiness/architecture-substrate
-> readiness/cost-resources
> The CLI is a thin adapter over the library and is not a second semantic authority.
> CLI input is a canonical or parseable Grimoire description plus explicit analysis inputs; labels and topology never supply resource assumptions.
> Human-readable and machine-readable output share one result model and stable failure boundary.
> The CLI remains offline and static; runtime training, sampling, controllers, and network operations are out of scope.
> The initial command surface is `validate`, `canonicalize`, `evaluate`, `cut`, and `resources`; resource scenarios use an explicit tab-delimited sidecar file.
> A CLI-produced viewer payload is preferred if the viewer generation gate chooses the generated path; direct opening remains a release requirement.
@artifact text/rust ./crates/grimoire/src/bin/grimoire.rs
@artifact text/rust ./crates/grimoire/tests/cli.rs
---
[readiness/mcp-detour] Defer the Grimoire MCP adapter (blocked)
Files: the release posture and public workflow boundary.
Do: Keep the Grimoire MCP adapter out of this release. Preserve the CLI and library as the only public machine-facing semantics, and keep the local Scry MCP integration private development evidence rather than adding an external service, dependency, configuration, or copied vocabulary to the repository.
Accept: The release posture names the Grimoire MCP adapter as deferred; the public tree contains no Grimoire MCP adapter or private Scry configuration; the two core workflows remain usable offline through the CLI; and a future adapter has a clear re-entry point after CLI request/result parity is designed.
-> readiness/cli
> The Grimoire MCP adapter is deferred from the current release, not a partially implemented core workflow.
> Any future adapter must reuse explicit description and analysis inputs rather than add a domain vocabulary.
> The current release remains offline and has no adapter credentials, hidden state, runtime execution, or network semantics.
> The deferred boundary is checked in release planning rather than by an adapter smoke test.
> Local Scry MCP is workspace-only development evidence, not a Grimoire runtime dependency or public repository configuration.
> Re-entry requires a stable CLI request/result contract and parity fixtures, without introducing a second semantic authority.
